From 5e7b08686437993d44705b734840021f32f6f4ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:31:29 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A one-page snapshot restore leaves blocks moved since in
 their new page

Restoring one page from a snapshot (RollbackRepoSnapshotFile, in patch
0008's turn) kept every block's id. A block moved from the page to another
page since the snapshot was then in both pages under one id: two rows in
the block record and two in SQL, reads by id naming the page it moved to,
and an edit by id saved there while the restored page kept the old text,
with nothing to heal it. A restore from history avoids this: it gives a
block the block record holds in another page a new id (SiYuan issue 14358).

In its turn, before it writes the page, the restore now gives a block of
the restored copy a new id when the block record places that id under
another page, by root id. The block stays in the page it moved to, with its
references, and the restored page holds the snapshot's copy as a block of
its own. A moved block's children, such as a list's items, are blocks too,
and every block gets its new id through treenode.ResetNodeID, as in the
history restore. The page block keeps its id, and so do the restored page's
own blocks and the blocks the block record doesn't hold, such as one
deleted since.

It compares pages, not notebooks, unlike the history restore's check, which
also renews the blocks of the page itself when the page now sits in another
notebook. So a page moved to another notebook since the snapshot restores
as before, and the history restore doesn't change.

The test, TestOnePageSnapshotRestoreLeavesBlocksMovedSinceInTheirNewPage in
restore_moved_blocks_fts5_test.go, through what SiYuan serves: two pages,
"Kitchen renovation" and "Sam"; a snapshot; a paragraph and a list with an
item moved from the first page to the second and another paragraph deleted,
as the editor sends them; then a restore of the first page. It checks that
the block record and SQL each hold one row for each moved id, under Sam's
page; that the restored page holds the snapshot's text under new ids, and
its own paragraph and the deleted one under their ids; and that an edit by
the moved paragraph's id changes Sam's page only. On v3.8.7 with patches
0001 to 0008 it fails: each moved id has a row in the block record and in
SQL under each page, and the restored page holds the moved blocks under
their old ids.

Checks run on the series: the test, TestSearchLeavesBlocksWhole and the
restore tests (TestRollback.*, .*SnapshotRestore.*, .*HistoryRestore.*)
pass with -count=1, 42 tests and subtests, and one skips as before, a gap the
official build has, recorded on polka-siyuan's #43; SiYuan's whole kernel
suite passes on the source, 35 packages; the image builds, applying 0001
to 0009, and runs every patch's tests.
